<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Girnar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Girnar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Girnar is a group of volcanic peaks near Junagadh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, sacred to Jains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. A rock at its foot carries inscriptions of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> Rudradaman and of the Gupta period. Its slopes hold Jain temples, including those dedicated to Neminatha and the shrines built by the ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastupala">Vastupala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tejapala">Tejapala</a> in the 13th century, and thousands of pilgrims climb its steps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/girnar.jpg" alt="Girnar" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sachinvenga · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dattatreya_Temple_of_Girnar.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Girnar is a hill in Junagadh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, administered by the Junagadh Municipal Corporation. Geologically it is a major igneous plutonic complex that intruded into the basalts towards the close of the Deccan Trap period, with rocks that include gabbros, diorites, lamprophyres, alkali-syenites and rhyolites. For Jains it is a Mahatirth, a great pilgrimage site, where Neminatha, the 22nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>, is believed to have attained omniscience and later nirvana at the highest peak, Neminath Shikhar, together with five hundred and thirty-three other enlightened sages, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra">Kalpa Sutra</a> of Acharya Bhadrabahu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fourteen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s Major Rock Edicts, dated to about 250 BCE, are cut on a large boulder outside the town of Junagadh, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> and a language similar to Pali; they mark the beginning of the written history of Junagadh. On the same rock the Western Satrap ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a> added a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription around 150 CE, which tells of Sudarshan Lake and the storm that broke it. Another, from about 450 CE, refers to the Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skandagupta">Skandagupta</a>. The Nawab Rasool Khan of Junagadh built a protective building around the rock in 1900.</p><p>Jain texts record Girnar as an ancient pilgrimage site from which countless monks attained liberation. Acharya Dharasena practised penance in the Candragumpha cave and taught Pushpadanta and Bhutabali, who wrote the Shatkhandagama between 86 CE and 156 CE. In the 7th century the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Hsuan-tsang</a> mentioned Girnar as a place of supernatural monks. Inscriptions at the temples record gifts and pilgrimages from the 12th century onward. One of 1232 CE states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastupala">Vastupala</a> built four temples with his own money, and those of 1230 CE on the Vastupala and Tejahpala temple praise Neminatha, Vastupala and Svetambara monks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Girnar holds a group of Svetambara Jain temples and one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> temple. The first peak carries the black granite temple of Neminatha, with carved pillars and coloured mosaics. The second peak has the temple of the goddess Ambika, and the third and fourth hold the foot idols of Muni Samba Kumar and Pradyumna Kumar. The fifth, reached by a climb of 10,000 steps, holds the foot idol of Neminatha.</p><p>Tradition says the present idol of Neminatha was installed in the 10th century by Ratnasar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, who is said to have received it from Ambika, and that Sajjana, a minister of Jaysimha Siddharaja, renovated the temple in the 12th century. A ropeway 2,320 metres long, opened on 24 October 2020, takes passengers to the Ambika temple in ten minutes. The main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> event is the Maha Shivaratri fair, held every year in the month of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga-magha">Magha</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Girnar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mahmud Begada's sobriquet Begada literally means the conqueror of two forts, probably after he took <b>Girnar</b> (Junagadh) and Champaner (Pavagadh).</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Pilgrimage to sacred places such as Shatrunjaya, <b>Mount Girnar</b> and Sammed Shikharji, where twenty of the Tirthankaras are said to have attained liberation, is a lifelong aspiration.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nawabs governed a mostly Hindu population that included the sacred <b>Girnar</b> hill and the temple of Somnath.</blockquote><a href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh State</a></li><li><blockquote>The ministers Vastupala and Tejapala, serving Viradhavala, are famous for building Jain temples at Mount Abu, notably the Luna Vasahi at Dilwara, and at <b>Girnar</b> and for their patronage of literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaghela-dynasty">Vaghela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
